<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00193</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b86a3591-9994-44fc-bc2c-cdca1dd73f6c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The perimeter of a rectangle is 48 centimeters. The relationship between the length, the width, and the perimeter of the rectangle can be described with the equation: \( 2 \cdot \text{length} +2 \cdot \text{width}=48 \).</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Find the length, in centimeters, if the width is:</para>
      <exercise id="_7ffc205b-b5c3-4b88-9038-cd798a83705e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">10 centimeters</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Correct! 14</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(2L + 2(10) = 48\) \(2L + 20 = 48\) \(2L + 20 = 48\) \(2L + 20 - 20 = 48 - 20\) \(2L + 20 - 20 = 48 - 20\) \(2L = 28\) \(2L = 28\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) =\frac{1}{2} (28)\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) =\frac{1}{2} (28)\) \(L = 14\) \(L = 14\)</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Nice try!</para>
          <para id="para-00007">\(2L + 2(10) = 48\) \(2L + 20 = 48\) \(2L + 20 = 48\) \(2L + 20 - 20 = 48 - 20\) \(2L + 20 - 20 = 48 - 20\) \(2L = 28\) \(2L = 28\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) =\frac{1}{2} (28)\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) =\frac{1}{2} (28)\) \(L = 14\) \(L = 14\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_07c3d8c2-ec3b-4992-90ee-3e264fc0822f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">3.6 centimeters</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Correct! 2</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\(2L + 2(3.6) = 48\) \(2L +7.2=48\) \(2L +7.2=48\) \(2L+7.2-7.2=48-7.2\) \(2L+7.2-7.2=48-7.2\) \(2L=40.8\) \(2L=40.8\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (40.8)\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (40.8)\) \(L=20.4\) \(L=20.4\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Nice try!</para>
          <para id="para-00012">\(2L + 2(3.6) = 48\) \(2L +7.2=48\) \(2L +7.2=48\) \(2L+7.2-7.2=48-7.2\) \(2L+7.2-7.2=48-7.2\) \(2L=40.8\) \(2L=40.8\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (40.8)\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (40.8)\) \(L=20.4\) \(L=20.4\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">\( w \) centimeters</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015">\(2L + 2(w) = 48\) \(2L + 2w = 48\) \(2L + 2w -2w= 48-2s\) \(2L = 48-2\) \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (48-2s)\) \(L=\frac{48-2w}{2}\) or \(24-w\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
